1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
And now it's the old scout master.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:15,640
It's the very first day of summer camp and one of my patrol leaders sees a scout walking

3
00:00:15,640 --> 00:00:19,440
down the trail limping a little bit, and he asked him if he was hurt. and the scout said:

4
00:00:19,440 --> 00:00:24,120
no, it's all these socks I'm wearing. and the patrol leader said: well, how many pairs

5
00:00:24,120 --> 00:00:27,800
of socks are you wearing? and the scout said: I'm wearing seven.

6
00:00:28,400 --> 00:00:32,800
Well, why in the world are you wearing seven pairs of socks?

7
00:00:32,800 --> 00:00:38,800
Well, the scout says it's to save time every morning.

8
00:00:38,800 --> 00:00:43,800
Well, how are you saving time every morning by wearing seven pairs of socks?

9
00:00:43,800 --> 00:00:49,800
And the scout looks at him and says: well, we're supposed to change socks every day, right,

10
00:00:49,800 --> 00:00:52,800
Yeah, you're supposed to change socks every day.

11
00:00:52,800 --> 00:00:56,800
Well, instead of going through all the trouble of changing socks every day,

12
00:00:56,800 --> 00:01:02,800
all I have to do is take one pair off every morning, and I'm good.

13
00:01:02,800 --> 00:01:05,800
I mean, it makes sense, doesn't it?

14
00:01:05,800 --> 00:01:08,800
You know, scouts are efficiency experts.

15
00:01:08,800 --> 00:01:11,800
You've got to hand it to them.

16
00:01:11,800 --> 00:01:15,800
Hey, this is podcast number 362..

17
00:01:15,800 --> 00:01:17,800
Hey,

18
00:01:23,800 --> 00:01:25,800
Hey,

19
00:01:42,800 --> 00:01:44,800
Welcome back to the Scoutmaster Podcast.

20
00:01:44,800 --> 00:01:50,800
This is Clark Green, and this is the second of a series of podcasts,

21
00:01:50,800 --> 00:01:57,800
whose number is yet to be determined, dealing with foundational stuff.

22
00:01:57,800 --> 00:02:03,800
Last week, in podcast 361, we talked about the allegory of scouting as a game.

23
00:02:03,800 --> 00:02:09,800
We're going to build on that today by talking about the role of the scouters in the game,

24
00:02:09,800 --> 00:02:14,800
And I had a number of great responses to last week's podcast

25
00:02:14,800 --> 00:02:15,800
and I really appreciate them.

26
00:02:15,800 --> 00:02:17,800
Thank you for your comments on Facebook.

27
00:02:17,800 --> 00:02:19,800
Thank you for the emails I received.

28
00:02:19,800 --> 00:02:23,800
I picked one out to share with you from Frank Maynard,

29
00:02:23,800 --> 00:02:29,800
who is the author of a blog at bobwhiteblathercom.

30
00:02:29,800 --> 00:02:31,800
It's exactly what you think it is:

31
00:02:31,800 --> 00:02:33,800
Bob White Blather.

32
00:02:33,800 --> 00:02:37,800
Frank is a long-tenured, very skilled scouter,

33
00:02:37,800 --> 00:02:40,800
Has a great blog that you ought to make sure to check out,

34
00:02:40,800 --> 00:02:44,800
and I'll have a link to Frank's blog in the podcast notes.

35
00:02:44,800 --> 00:02:46,800
But he wrote this email to me.

36
00:02:46,800 --> 00:02:50,800
He said I listened to podcast 331, where you said

37
00:02:50,800 --> 00:02:52,800
the organization is not the game.

38
00:02:52,800 --> 00:02:55,800
I think too many people missed this point

39
00:02:55,800 --> 00:02:59,800
and think that the BSA defines scouting and not the other way around.

40
00:02:59,800 --> 00:03:02,800
Scouting defines itself by the oath and law

41
00:03:02,800 --> 00:03:05,800
and the BSA gives us the framework to do scouting.

42
00:03:05,800 --> 00:03:08,800
I found the basketball analogy was rather a good one.

43
00:03:08,800 --> 00:03:09,800
Well, thank you.

44
00:03:09,800 --> 00:03:13,800
The foregoing opens up another analogy.

45
00:03:13,800 --> 00:03:15,800
So you ready for another analogy, folks,

46
00:03:15,800 --> 00:03:17,800
Because I think this is a good one too.

47
00:03:17,800 --> 00:03:20,800
A scouting organization can provide the framework for scouting,

48
00:03:20,800 --> 00:03:22,800
but it can't contain everything that scouting is,

49
00:03:22,800 --> 00:03:26,800
because it simply can't fully reduce a concept,

50
00:03:26,800 --> 00:03:31,800
a movement, down to a set of rules, regulations and procedures.

51
00:03:31,800 --> 00:03:36,800
This is much the same way as a system defining a color space

52
00:03:36,800 --> 00:03:39,800
cannot contain all possible colors.

53
00:03:39,800 --> 00:03:42,800
I deal with this in engineering color television systems,

54
00:03:42,800 --> 00:03:45,800
where we try to reproduce a world in color

55
00:03:45,800 --> 00:03:49,800
using just red, green and blue vectors.

56
00:03:49,800 --> 00:03:52,800
We employ what's called the CIE color space.

57
00:03:52,800 --> 00:03:54,800
It looks like every imaginable color,

58
00:03:54,800 --> 00:03:58,800
but if you look closely you'll see that some colors are just missing.

59
00:03:58,800 --> 00:04:03,800
Some tones are particularly difficult to reproduce with those limitations.

60
00:04:03,800 --> 00:04:06,800
in the same way that scouting is not reproducible

61
00:04:06,800 --> 00:04:09,800
from an organizational standpoint,

62
00:04:09,800 --> 00:04:12,800
While we need to follow the BSA's rules

63
00:04:12,800 --> 00:04:15,800
when we implement scouting under its framework,

64
00:04:15,800 --> 00:04:17,800
we need to remember that aspects of scouting

65
00:04:17,800 --> 00:04:21,800
are more vivid than what we can get from an organization.

66
00:04:21,800 --> 00:04:24,800
Well, Frank, those are excellent thoughts

67
00:04:24,800 --> 00:04:27,800
and they do a good job of giving everybody another perspective

68
00:04:27,800 --> 00:04:29,800
on the issues I was working with last week.

69
00:04:29,800 --> 00:04:30,800
I really appreciate it.

70
00:04:30,800 --> 00:04:33,800
As advertised, we're just going to soldier right into

71
00:04:33,800 --> 00:04:37,800
our next foundational series, in which we'll be talking about

72
00:04:37,800 --> 00:04:39,800
the role of the coaches in the game.

73
00:04:39,800 --> 00:04:41,800
And that's us, That's the Scouters.

74
00:04:41,800 --> 00:04:43,800
So let's get started, shall we?

75
00:04:43,800 --> 00:05:06,800
Music.

76
00:05:06,800 --> 00:05:10,800
In the last podcast we talked about scouting as a game

77
00:05:10,800 --> 00:05:13,800
with a goal, and that's the development of character

78
00:05:13,800 --> 00:05:14,800
within the individual.

79
00:05:14,800 --> 00:05:17,800
We define character as those ideals expressed

80
00:05:17,800 --> 00:05:19,800
in the Scout Oath and Law.

81
00:05:19,800 --> 00:05:22,800
So we have players- the Scouts- and coaches.

82
00:05:22,800 --> 00:05:24,800
That's us, the Scouters.

83
00:05:24,800 --> 00:05:26,800
We have a set of rules defining the game

84
00:05:26,800 --> 00:05:28,800
and at the intersection of the rules,

85
00:05:28,800 --> 00:05:30,800
the players and coaches, is something

86
00:05:30,800 --> 00:05:32,800
we're going to call strategy.

87
00:05:32,800 --> 00:05:34,800
Remember, our game isn't competitive.

88
00:05:34,800 --> 00:05:37,800
Our game does not end in a zero-sum win or loss.

89
00:05:37,800 --> 00:05:40,800
Our game is kind of like assembling puzzles.

90
00:05:40,800 --> 00:05:42,800
There's another analogy:

91
00:05:42,800 --> 00:05:45,800
Our game is helping individual human beings

92
00:05:45,800 --> 00:05:47,800
to shape and define their lives.

93
00:05:47,800 --> 00:05:50,800
So today I want to talk about the role of Scouters

94
00:05:50,800 --> 00:05:52,800
as coaches in the game.

95
00:05:52,800 --> 00:05:56,800
Now, remember, you know we're using analogies, allegories.

96
00:05:56,800 --> 00:05:58,800
Remember, you're not really a coach

97
00:05:58,800 --> 00:06:00,800
and scouting is not really a game.

98
00:06:00,800 --> 00:06:04,800
Analogies are useful for taking something we understand

99
00:06:04,800 --> 00:06:07,800
and applying them to know something

100
00:06:07,800 --> 00:06:09,800
that we don't understand.

101
00:06:09,800 --> 00:06:11,800
They're not exact copies of each other,

102
00:06:11,800 --> 00:06:13,800
They just resemble each other.

103
00:06:13,800 --> 00:06:16,800
Coaches and Scouters may do many of the same things,

104
00:06:16,800 --> 00:06:19,800
but their roles are different in some ways too.

105
00:06:19,800 --> 00:06:21,800
We talked a bit about how our game

106
00:06:21,800 --> 00:06:23,800
works in the last podcast.

107
00:06:23,800 --> 00:06:25,800
Everything important to achieving our goal

108
00:06:25,800 --> 00:06:27,800
happens at the small group level

109
00:06:27,800 --> 00:06:31,800
and everything important happens to individual Scouts.

110
00:06:31,800 --> 00:06:34,800
To make this happen, we have some basic tools

111
00:06:34,800 --> 00:06:36,800
and methods we apply to our roles,

112
00:06:36,800 --> 00:06:39,800
and the tools I want to talk about are perspective,

113
00:06:39,800 --> 00:06:42,800
observation, dialogue, discovery

114
00:06:42,800 --> 00:06:44,800
and responsive application.

115
00:06:44,800 --> 00:06:46,800
So that's not too bad right?

116
00:06:46,800 --> 00:06:49,800
Not too many $9 college words floating around there.

117
00:06:49,800 --> 00:06:51,800
Let's begin by talking about perspective,

118
00:06:51,800 --> 00:06:53,800
because it is the first and most important aspect

119
00:06:53,800 --> 00:06:55,800
of understanding your role as a Scouter.

120
00:06:55,800 --> 00:06:57,800
Being a Scouter, as we've been saying,

121
00:06:57,800 --> 00:06:59,800
is like a lot of things,

122
00:06:59,800 --> 00:07:01,800
but it's not actually those things,

123
00:07:01,800 --> 00:07:03,800
because Scouters hold a unique role

124
00:07:03,800 --> 00:07:05,800
in a young person's life.

125
00:07:05,800 --> 00:07:09,800
They're part parent, coach, counselor, mentor and friend.

126
00:07:09,800 --> 00:07:11,800
Baden Powell put it this way.

127
00:07:11,800 --> 00:07:13,800
He said the Scoutmaster has to be

128
00:07:13,800 --> 00:07:16,800
neither schoolmaster nor commanding officer,

129
00:07:16,800 --> 00:07:18,800
nor pastor nor instructor,

130
00:07:18,800 --> 00:07:20,800
And he went on to say:

131
00:07:20,800 --> 00:07:22,800
all that is needed is the capacity

132
00:07:22,800 --> 00:07:24,800
to enjoy the outdoors,

133
00:07:24,800 --> 00:07:26,800
to enter into the Scouts' ambitions,

134
00:07:26,800 --> 00:07:28,800
to put himself on the level of the Scouts,

135
00:07:28,800 --> 00:07:31,800
That is, to see things from the Scouts' point of view

136
00:07:31,800 --> 00:07:34,800
and to lead and guide and give enthusiasm

137
00:07:34,800 --> 00:07:36,800
in the right direction.

138
00:07:36,800 --> 00:07:39,800
So we know that a Scouter is kind of like a coach

139
00:07:39,800 --> 00:07:42,800
and we know that a Scouter is not a commanding officer

140
00:07:42,800 --> 00:07:44,800
and Scouts are not soldiers.

141
00:07:44,800 --> 00:07:46,800
A Scouter is not the boss of a company

142
00:07:46,800 --> 00:07:49,800
and Scouts are not their employees.

143
00:07:49,800 --> 00:07:51,800
A Scouter is not a teacher

144
00:07:51,800 --> 00:07:53,800
and Scouts are not students.

145
00:07:53,800 --> 00:07:55,800
A Scouter is not a pastor

146
00:07:55,800 --> 00:07:58,800
and Scouts are not members of the pastor's congregation.

147
00:07:58,800 --> 00:08:00,800
We're going to talk about our role

148
00:08:00,800 --> 00:08:03,800
in the terms of the game analogy, as coaches,

149
00:08:03,800 --> 00:08:06,800
but remember- I just want to stress this-

150
00:08:06,800 --> 00:08:08,800
we're not really coaches.

151
00:08:08,800 --> 00:08:11,800
Hopefully it's a helpful analogy,

152
00:08:11,800 --> 00:08:14,800
but analogies all break down at some point.

153
00:08:14,800 --> 00:08:16,800
Let's see if we can push this one

154
00:08:16,800 --> 00:08:18,800
to the breaking point today. okay,

155
00:08:18,800 --> 00:08:21,800
So the tools we employ to carry out this role

156
00:08:21,800 --> 00:08:23,800
are deceptively simple to explain

157
00:08:23,800 --> 00:08:25,800
but take a lifetime to master.

158
00:08:25,800 --> 00:08:28,800
It's easy to explain a game of golf.

159
00:08:28,800 --> 00:08:30,800
You take a ball and a stick

160
00:08:30,800 --> 00:08:32,799
and there's a hole out there in the distance

161
00:08:32,799 --> 00:08:35,799
and you keep hitting the ball until you get it in the hole.

162
00:08:35,799 --> 00:08:38,799
Easy to explain, but to actually get good at that game,

163
00:08:38,799 --> 00:08:40,799
well, you know what it's going to take, right,

164
00:08:40,799 --> 00:08:43,799
It really is that simple to describe.

165
00:08:43,799 --> 00:08:46,800
but don't let that simplicity lull you into the idea

166
00:08:46,800 --> 00:08:48,800
that you become an immediate master

167
00:08:48,800 --> 00:08:51,800
of those simple things once you understand what they are.

168
00:08:51,800 --> 00:08:53,800
It takes a lot of practice

169
00:08:53,800 --> 00:08:55,800
and that's half the fun of playing the game.

170
00:08:55,800 --> 00:08:57,800
So now that we have our perspective down.

171
00:08:57,800 --> 00:08:59,800
we know kind of what we are and what we aren't.

172
00:08:59,800 --> 00:09:02,800
Let's talk about observation, dialogue, discovery

173
00:09:02,800 --> 00:09:04,800
and responsive application.

174
00:09:04,800 --> 00:09:06,800
Since our goal is individual,

175
00:09:06,800 --> 00:09:09,800
we have to understand our scouts on an individual level.

176
00:09:09,800 --> 00:09:11,800
We can't process individuals

177
00:09:11,800 --> 00:09:13,800
like we process widgets on an assembly line.

178
00:09:13,800 --> 00:09:15,800
To know who they are as an individual,

179
00:09:15,800 --> 00:09:17,800
we have to use our powers of observation

180
00:09:17,800 --> 00:09:20,800
And to understand the dynamics

181
00:09:20,800 --> 00:09:22,800
of small and large groups.

182
00:09:22,800 --> 00:09:24,800
we have to observe them working together,

183
00:09:24,800 --> 00:09:28,800
And observing is kind of an underrated tool a lot of times.

184
00:09:28,800 --> 00:09:30,800
Now I'm going to make a slight digression,

185
00:09:30,800 --> 00:09:32,800
but an important point.

186
00:09:32,800 --> 00:09:34,800
If you accept the premise that I built here-

187
00:09:34,800 --> 00:09:36,800
our scouts advance towards the goal

188
00:09:36,800 --> 00:09:39,800
on an individual and a small group level-

189
00:09:39,800 --> 00:09:43,800
You immediately recognize that scouting has a scale.

190
00:09:43,800 --> 00:09:46,800
Now Baden Powell talked about patrols of eight or so scouts

191
00:09:46,800 --> 00:09:49,800
because he understood that too far below

192
00:09:49,800 --> 00:09:51,800
or too far above that number

193
00:09:51,800 --> 00:09:53,800
things don't work as they ought to.

194
00:09:53,800 --> 00:09:56,800
Baden Powell also warned against scouts

195
00:09:56,800 --> 00:09:59,800
getting lost in herds.

196
00:09:59,800 --> 00:10:01,800
He recommended that troops shouldn't be much larger

197
00:10:01,800 --> 00:10:03,800
than 40 or 50 scouts

198
00:10:03,800 --> 00:10:05,800
because beyond that number

199
00:10:05,800 --> 00:10:07,800
a scouter is challenged to understand

200
00:10:07,800 --> 00:10:10,800
and work with scouts on an individual basis.

201
00:10:10,800 --> 00:10:12,800
End of slight digression.

202
00:10:12,800 --> 00:10:14,800
Go back to talking about observation.

203
00:10:14,800 --> 00:10:17,800
Observation is a real important aspect of our work

204
00:10:17,800 --> 00:10:21,800
because it involves watching and listening

205
00:10:21,800 --> 00:10:24,800
without responding or interfering.

206
00:10:24,800 --> 00:10:26,800
To introduce another allegory,

207
00:10:26,800 --> 00:10:28,800
take a look at this as a first aid problem.

208
00:10:28,800 --> 00:10:30,800
If you're going to render effective first aid,

209
00:10:30,800 --> 00:10:33,800
you first have to observe and diagnose.

210
00:10:33,800 --> 00:10:35,800
And if there are several different injuries going on,

211
00:10:35,800 --> 00:10:37,800
we have to triage the situation

212
00:10:37,800 --> 00:10:39,800
and treat the most dire injuries first.

213
00:10:39,800 --> 00:10:42,800
No matter how well we bandage someone's ankle,

214
00:10:42,800 --> 00:10:45,800
we're not doing them much good if they aren't breathing right.

215
00:10:45,800 --> 00:10:48,800
So the importance of observation is lost on many of us

216
00:10:48,800 --> 00:10:52,800
because we're really anxious to apply our skills and knowledge.

217
00:10:52,800 --> 00:10:54,800
We're anxious to play the game,

218
00:10:54,800 --> 00:10:58,800
We're anxious to start talking about strategies

219
00:10:58,800 --> 00:11:01,800
and start training players and things like that.

220
00:11:01,800 --> 00:11:03,800
But we have to observe,

221
00:11:03,800 --> 00:11:05,800
because applying any of the skills that we might have

222
00:11:05,800 --> 00:11:08,800
to a situation without first observing it thoroughly

223
00:11:08,800 --> 00:11:11,800
is an exercise in frustration and futility.

224
00:11:11,800 --> 00:11:13,800
So here's an observation exercise for you.

225
00:11:13,800 --> 00:11:17,800
Put a chair in your troop meeting room,

226
00:11:17,800 --> 00:11:19,800
sit in the chair

227
00:11:19,800 --> 00:11:22,800
and don't say anything for an entire troop meeting.

228
00:11:22,800 --> 00:11:25,800
Give that a shot. It's next to impossible

229
00:11:25,800 --> 00:11:27,800
if you're like me.

230
00:11:27,800 --> 00:11:29,800
But see what you observe,

231
00:11:29,800 --> 00:11:31,800
Take notes,

232
00:11:31,800 --> 00:11:33,800
Pretend you're a biologist.

233
00:11:33,800 --> 00:11:35,800
That's the kind of observation I'm talking about,

234
00:11:35,800 --> 00:11:37,800
One where we're not offering advice,

235
00:11:37,800 --> 00:11:39,800
where we don't interfere

236
00:11:39,800 --> 00:11:41,800
but we're just watching to try and understand

237
00:11:41,800 --> 00:11:42,800
what's going on.

238
00:11:42,800 --> 00:11:44,800
The next tool that we're going to use

239
00:11:44,800 --> 00:11:47,800
after we've observed is dialogue.

240
00:11:47,800 --> 00:11:49,800
Observing will draw the broader lines

241
00:11:49,800 --> 00:11:51,800
of understanding about the situation.

242
00:11:51,800 --> 00:11:53,800
We fill in the details with dialogue.

243
00:11:53,800 --> 00:11:56,800
So back to our first date analogy for a moment.

244
00:11:56,800 --> 00:11:58,800
We see someone lying on the ground,

245
00:11:58,800 --> 00:11:59,800
maybe their arm or leg-

246
00:11:59,800 --> 00:12:01,800
Is it an unusual angle?

247
00:12:01,800 --> 00:12:03,800
So once the scene is secure,

248
00:12:03,800 --> 00:12:05,800
we talk to them and ask them what happened

249
00:12:05,800 --> 00:12:07,800
and what discomfort they're experiencing,

250
00:12:07,800 --> 00:12:10,800
and we listen to their answers,

251
00:12:10,800 --> 00:12:13,800
And that's before we apply any kind of first aid.

252
00:12:13,800 --> 00:12:16,800
Dialogue in our context is a conversation

253
00:12:16,800 --> 00:12:19,800
where we ask questions and listen to answers.

254
00:12:19,800 --> 00:12:21,800
It's really that simple.

255
00:12:21,800 --> 00:12:24,800
As a scouter, dialogue is one of your most powerful tools.

256
00:12:24,800 --> 00:12:27,800
We can break dialogue down into two basic streams:

257
00:12:27,800 --> 00:12:30,800
Inquiry and direction.

258
00:12:30,800 --> 00:12:33,800
Inquiry employs open-ended questions,

259
00:12:33,800 --> 00:12:37,800
Ones that require more than a yes or a no answer,

260
00:12:37,800 --> 00:12:39,800
And that leads our scouts to explain

261
00:12:39,800 --> 00:12:43,800
what they understand about a given idea or situation.

262
00:12:43,800 --> 00:12:45,800
And when they explain what they understand,

263
00:12:45,800 --> 00:12:48,800
we then know what they don't understand,

264
00:12:48,800 --> 00:12:51,800
or we understand their perspective of things.

265
00:12:51,800 --> 00:12:54,800
And direction is just what it sounds like:

266
00:12:54,800 --> 00:12:56,800
Giving directions or making statements

267
00:12:56,800 --> 00:12:58,800
rather than asking questions.

268
00:12:58,800 --> 00:13:00,800
Now, if we're honest with ourselves,

269
00:13:00,800 --> 00:13:03,800
our first response in most situations is directive

270
00:13:03,800 --> 00:13:05,800
And there's good reason for that.

271
00:13:05,800 --> 00:13:07,800
Direction creates immediate results. we hope,

272
00:13:07,800 --> 00:13:09,800
It's efficient

273
00:13:09,800 --> 00:13:11,800
And it's an expression of our authority,

274
00:13:11,800 --> 00:13:13,800
and all of those things make us feel good.

275
00:13:13,800 --> 00:13:15,800
On the other side of the equation,

276
00:13:15,800 --> 00:13:17,800
direction does not do a whole lot

277
00:13:17,800 --> 00:13:19,800
to advance us towards the goal

278
00:13:19,800 --> 00:13:22,800
unless it leads to a shared understanding

279
00:13:22,800 --> 00:13:24,800
of why the direction was given.

280
00:13:24,800 --> 00:13:26,800
Now I hope you're following me,

281
00:13:26,800 --> 00:13:28,800
because this is both really important

282
00:13:28,800 --> 00:13:30,800
and it's kind of tiresome to talk about

283
00:13:30,800 --> 00:13:32,800
and to listen to.

284
00:13:32,800 --> 00:13:34,800
After all, who has the time to go through all this?

285
00:13:34,800 --> 00:13:36,800
Tell our scouts what to do and get it done.

286
00:13:36,800 --> 00:13:38,800
I mean, my dad used to put it this way.

287
00:13:38,800 --> 00:13:40,800
When I say jump you jump.

288
00:13:40,800 --> 00:13:42,800
I bet you there are plenty of you

289
00:13:42,800 --> 00:13:44,800
who are familiar with that particular saying.

290
00:13:44,800 --> 00:13:46,800
Now, that's an effective way to produce

291
00:13:46,800 --> 00:13:48,800
immediate results and to foster discipline.

292
00:13:48,800 --> 00:13:50,800
but immediate results and discipline

293
00:13:50,800 --> 00:13:53,800
are very thin slices of our overall goal.

294
00:13:53,800 --> 00:13:55,800
So keep track of how many times

295
00:13:55,800 --> 00:13:58,800
you respond to a situation by giving directions.

296
00:13:58,800 --> 00:14:01,800
Then consider how you can use those situations

297
00:14:01,800 --> 00:14:03,800
to apply inquiry

298
00:14:03,800 --> 00:14:05,800
and build character in your scouts

299
00:14:05,800 --> 00:14:07,800
by advancing the scout oath and law.

300
00:14:07,800 --> 00:14:10,800
You'll do that more through inquiry

301
00:14:10,800 --> 00:14:12,800
and dialogue than through giving directions.

302
00:14:12,800 --> 00:14:14,800
And remember this dialogue,

303
00:14:14,800 --> 00:14:16,800
this inquiry that we're doing

304
00:14:16,800 --> 00:14:19,800
is intended to get scouts to direct themselves

305
00:14:19,800 --> 00:14:21,800
by asking them questions.

306
00:14:21,800 --> 00:14:23,800
I'm not saying giving directions

307
00:14:23,800 --> 00:14:25,800
is always wrong or misplaced,

308
00:14:25,800 --> 00:14:28,800
Just that it's not as important as it seems to be

309
00:14:28,800 --> 00:14:31,800
to get ourselves between point A and B

310
00:14:31,800 --> 00:14:33,800
to invoke the process of scouting.

311
00:14:33,800 --> 00:14:35,800
Here's another analogy: you ready?

312
00:14:35,800 --> 00:14:38,800
So imagine a bus tour

313
00:14:38,800 --> 00:14:41,800
of you know. let's say it's some important city

314
00:14:41,800 --> 00:14:44,800
and we're all welcomed into

315
00:14:44,800 --> 00:14:47,800
and seated in the air-conditioned comfort of a motor coach

316
00:14:47,800 --> 00:14:50,800
and a guide stands up front with a microphone

317
00:14:50,800 --> 00:14:52,800
and we listen to the guide explain each landmark

318
00:14:52,800 --> 00:14:55,800
in detail so we won't miss anything.

319
00:14:55,800 --> 00:14:57,800
The guide sticks to the script.

320
00:14:57,800 --> 00:14:59,800
We sit behind the tinted windows of our bus,

321
00:14:59,800 --> 00:15:01,800
dutifully turning our heads to the left

322
00:15:01,800 --> 00:15:03,800
and then to the right.

323
00:15:03,800 --> 00:15:05,800
There's so much explaining going on

324
00:15:05,800 --> 00:15:08,800
that there's not much time left for questions,

325
00:15:08,800 --> 00:15:10,800
and soon the tour is over.

326
00:15:10,800 --> 00:15:12,800
Now contrast that bus tour with a hike

327
00:15:12,800 --> 00:15:14,800
that's led by a knowledgeable guide.

328
00:15:14,800 --> 00:15:16,800
He takes up the rear of the group,

329
00:15:16,800 --> 00:15:18,800
lets the group find the trail.

330
00:15:18,800 --> 00:15:20,800
When the path branches,

331
00:15:20,800 --> 00:15:22,800
they'll tell us what to do.

332
00:15:22,800 --> 00:15:24,800
if we can't figure it out on our own,

333
00:15:24,800 --> 00:15:26,800
They don't mind if we stop now and then

334
00:15:26,800 --> 00:15:28,800
we're taking the tour or taking the view,

335
00:15:28,800 --> 00:15:30,800
and they'll happily tell us all about

336
00:15:30,800 --> 00:15:32,800
what we're looking at. if we ask

337
00:15:32,800 --> 00:15:34,800
Now, that type of guide is not necessarily

338
00:15:34,800 --> 00:15:36,800
going to volunteer much information.

339
00:15:36,800 --> 00:15:38,800
They might drop a hint here or there

340
00:15:38,800 --> 00:15:40,800
and they'll answer any questions we have,

341
00:15:40,800 --> 00:15:42,800
and we may actually

342
00:15:42,800 --> 00:15:44,800
miss some sites along the way

343
00:15:44,800 --> 00:15:46,800
or pass by interesting things,

344
00:15:46,800 --> 00:15:48,800
but our group will probably get more out

345
00:15:48,800 --> 00:15:50,800
of what we discovered along the hike

346
00:15:50,800 --> 00:15:52,800
and asked about

347
00:15:52,800 --> 00:15:54,800
than what the guide in the bus tour

348
00:15:54,800 --> 00:15:56,800
told us about.

349
00:15:56,800 --> 00:15:58,800
So working with Scouts is more like

350
00:15:58,800 --> 00:16:00,800
the hike than the bus tour:

351
00:16:00,800 --> 00:16:02,800
A gentle push in the right direction

352
00:16:02,800 --> 00:16:04,800
rather than dragging them along.

353
00:16:04,800 --> 00:16:06,800
A suggestion rather than a command,

354
00:16:06,800 --> 00:16:08,800
A question asked rather than

355
00:16:08,800 --> 00:16:10,800
an answer given.

356
00:16:10,800 --> 00:16:12,800
Our role is more responsive

357
00:16:12,800 --> 00:16:14,800
than directed.

358
00:16:14,800 --> 00:16:16,800
Each group of Scouts is different,

359
00:16:16,800 --> 00:16:18,800
so how we play out our role

360
00:16:18,800 --> 00:16:20,800
is in response

361
00:16:20,800 --> 00:16:22,800
to their development.

362
00:16:22,800 --> 00:16:24,800
so we have a perspective,

363
00:16:24,800 --> 00:16:26,800
we observe.

364
00:16:26,800 --> 00:16:28,800
from that perspective,

365
00:16:28,800 --> 00:16:30,800
we enter into dialogue which balances

366
00:16:30,800 --> 00:16:32,800
inquiry and direction,

367
00:16:32,800 --> 00:16:34,800
and this results in discovery

368
00:16:34,800 --> 00:16:36,800
for our Scouts.

369
00:16:36,800 --> 00:16:38,800
They discover

370
00:16:38,800 --> 00:16:40,800
what to do next, They discover

371
00:16:40,800 --> 00:16:42,800
the next steps forward

372
00:16:42,800 --> 00:16:44,800
because, think about it, it works this way:

373
00:16:44,800 --> 00:16:46,800
We observe our observations and form our dialogue.

374
00:16:46,800 --> 00:16:48,800
we inquire

375
00:16:48,800 --> 00:16:50,800
of the Scouts what they understand

376
00:16:50,800 --> 00:16:52,800
and they discover what they understand

377
00:16:52,800 --> 00:16:54,800
and what they don't understand.

378
00:16:54,800 --> 00:16:56,800
If we aren't asking questions

379
00:16:56,800 --> 00:16:58,800
and we simply give directions.

380
00:16:58,800 --> 00:17:00,800
it will kind of work

381
00:17:00,800 --> 00:17:02,800
if we go back

382
00:17:02,800 --> 00:17:04,800
and then make inquiries about

383
00:17:04,800 --> 00:17:06,800
why we gave the particular directions that we did

384
00:17:06,800 --> 00:17:08,800
so that Scouts will understand

385
00:17:08,800 --> 00:17:10,800
the basis of those directions.

386
00:17:10,800 --> 00:17:12,800
but I favor

387
00:17:12,800 --> 00:17:14,800
the first way

388
00:17:14,800 --> 00:17:16,800
because Scouts will end up directing themselves

389
00:17:16,800 --> 00:17:18,800
and they will discover things

390
00:17:18,800 --> 00:17:20,800
and that process will encourage them

391
00:17:20,800 --> 00:17:22,800
to keep on discovering.

392
00:17:22,800 --> 00:17:24,800
I'm a big advocate

393
00:17:24,800 --> 00:17:26,800
of inquiry, of asking questions

394
00:17:26,800 --> 00:17:28,800
rather than giving directions.

395
00:17:28,800 --> 00:17:30,800
so all of these things-

396
00:17:30,800 --> 00:17:32,800
the perspective, the observation,

397
00:17:32,800 --> 00:17:34,800
the dialogue, the discovery-

398
00:17:34,800 --> 00:17:36,800
now put us in the place

399
00:17:36,800 --> 00:17:38,800
where we can wisely

400
00:17:38,800 --> 00:17:40,800
respond to what our Scouts need

401
00:17:40,800 --> 00:17:42,800
and we can

402
00:17:42,800 --> 00:17:44,800
apply some of the rules of the game,

403
00:17:44,800 --> 00:17:46,800
some of the methods, some of the strategies.

404
00:17:46,800 --> 00:17:48,800
each group of Scouts is different,

405
00:17:48,800 --> 00:17:50,800
so how we play our roll out

406
00:17:50,800 --> 00:17:52,800
is a response to their development,

407
00:17:52,800 --> 00:17:54,800
their groups, dynamics and their abilities.

408
00:17:54,800 --> 00:17:56,800
we learn about who they are,

409
00:17:56,800 --> 00:17:58,800
what they're able to do,

410
00:17:58,800 --> 00:18:00,800
the pace of their development.

411
00:18:00,800 --> 00:18:02,800
we talk about it, we ask them questions about it.

412
00:18:02,800 --> 00:18:04,800
they discover things,

413
00:18:04,800 --> 00:18:06,800
we discover things, and this sets the pace

414
00:18:06,800 --> 00:18:08,800
of our work.

415
00:18:08,800 --> 00:18:10,800
Individuals and groups understand

416
00:18:10,800 --> 00:18:12,800
and apply concepts at differing paces.

417
00:18:12,800 --> 00:18:14,800
each has their own starting point.

418
00:18:14,800 --> 00:18:16,800
our job is focused on

419
00:18:16,800 --> 00:18:18,800
advancing them forward from where they started.

420
00:18:18,800 --> 00:18:20,800
now I know it sounds

421
00:18:20,800 --> 00:18:22,800
too simple, and it probably is,

422
00:18:22,800 --> 00:18:24,800
but observation, dialogue,

423
00:18:24,800 --> 00:18:26,800
discovery and responsive

424
00:18:26,800 --> 00:18:28,800
application are the tools we apply

425
00:18:28,800 --> 00:18:30,800
in this endlessly recursive

426
00:18:30,800 --> 00:18:32,800
process that never ends.

427
00:18:32,800 --> 00:18:34,800
it is who Scouts are

428
00:18:34,800 --> 00:18:36,800
and what we do. I'm putting it in simple terms,

429
00:18:36,800 --> 00:18:38,800
but it's endlessly complex

430
00:18:38,800 --> 00:18:40,800
when you begin to practice it.

431
00:18:40,800 --> 00:18:42,800
so the last thing I want to talk about today

432
00:18:42,800 --> 00:18:44,800
is attitude. All of what we're doing

433
00:18:44,800 --> 00:18:46,800
comes under the umbrella

434
00:18:46,800 --> 00:18:48,800
of the attitude with which

435
00:18:48,800 --> 00:18:50,800
we approach what we're doing.

436
00:18:50,800 --> 00:18:52,800
employing our skills from the right perspective

437
00:18:52,800 --> 00:18:54,800
is important, but they won't achieve

438
00:18:54,800 --> 00:18:56,800
that goal with out this

439
00:18:56,800 --> 00:18:58,800
final piece of the puzzle,

440
00:18:58,800 --> 00:19:00,800
and that's having the right attitude.

441
00:19:00,800 --> 00:19:02,800
Attitude is a choice,

442
00:19:02,800 --> 00:19:04,800
and this choice is what makes the roll

443
00:19:04,800 --> 00:19:06,800
of a Scouter open to just about anybody.

444
00:19:06,800 --> 00:19:08,800
we don't have to really

445
00:19:08,800 --> 00:19:10,800
go into a complex definition

446
00:19:10,800 --> 00:19:12,800
of what the right attitude is,

447
00:19:12,800 --> 00:19:14,800
because it's defined by the Scout

448
00:19:14,800 --> 00:19:16,800
Oath and Law. That may sound simplistic,

449
00:19:16,800 --> 00:19:18,800
but it's exactly right

450
00:19:18,800 --> 00:19:20,800
and it's one of the only things I know

451
00:19:20,800 --> 00:19:22,800
is exactly right, because when I examine

452
00:19:22,800 --> 00:19:24,800
my own attitude about things

453
00:19:24,800 --> 00:19:26,800
and I examine that against the ideals

454
00:19:26,800 --> 00:19:28,800
of the Scout Oath and Law,

455
00:19:28,800 --> 00:19:30,800
things become very clear

456
00:19:30,800 --> 00:19:32,800
indeed. I haven't always been,

457
00:19:32,800 --> 00:19:34,800
am not now and certainly

458
00:19:34,800 --> 00:19:36,800
will never become a perfect

459
00:19:36,800 --> 00:19:38,800
expression of the Scout Oath and Law,

460
00:19:38,800 --> 00:19:40,800
but I'm going to keep trying

461
00:19:40,800 --> 00:19:42,800
and encourage my Scouts to do the same.

462
00:19:42,800 --> 00:19:44,800
so, in short,

463
00:19:44,800 --> 00:19:46,800
I'm going to be compassionate.

464
00:19:46,800 --> 00:19:48,800
I'm going to strive to understand

465
00:19:48,800 --> 00:19:50,800
and relieve the flaws, sufferings

466
00:19:50,800 --> 00:19:52,800
and misfortunes of others,

467
00:19:52,800 --> 00:19:54,800
and I can't do that in others

468
00:19:54,800 --> 00:19:56,800
without first accepting

469
00:19:56,800 --> 00:19:58,800
and relieving them in myself.

470
00:19:58,800 --> 00:20:00,800
so that's an important aspect

471
00:20:00,800 --> 00:20:02,800
of the right attitude. right

472
00:20:02,800 --> 00:20:04,800
is not just being kind

473
00:20:04,800 --> 00:20:06,800
and considerate and compassionate to others,

474
00:20:06,800 --> 00:20:08,800
but finding considerate and compassionate

475
00:20:08,800 --> 00:20:10,800
to yourself. In the last podcast

476
00:20:10,800 --> 00:20:12,800
I talked about games being

477
00:20:12,800 --> 00:20:14,800
theoretically perfectable

478
00:20:14,800 --> 00:20:16,800
but practically imperfectable,

479
00:20:16,800 --> 00:20:18,800
and our attitudes are kind of the same way

480
00:20:18,800 --> 00:20:20,800
as our people.

481
00:20:20,800 --> 00:20:22,800
we are the same way as people.

482
00:20:22,800 --> 00:20:24,800
we are theoretically perfectable,

483
00:20:24,800 --> 00:20:26,800
but practically

484
00:20:26,800 --> 00:20:28,800
nobody's going to be a perfect person.

485
00:20:28,800 --> 00:20:30,800
we may understand the ideals

486
00:20:30,800 --> 00:20:32,800
in the Scout Oath and Law,

487
00:20:32,800 --> 00:20:34,800
but we're not going to be a perfect representative

488
00:20:34,800 --> 00:20:36,800
100% of the time.

489
00:20:36,800 --> 00:20:38,800
these two ideas present us

490
00:20:38,800 --> 00:20:40,800
with an aggravating conflict

491
00:20:40,800 --> 00:20:42,800
because they create what we called

492
00:20:42,800 --> 00:20:44,800
in the last podcast cognitive dissonance.

493
00:20:44,800 --> 00:20:46,800
Scouters need to learn to accept

494
00:20:46,800 --> 00:20:48,800
that this dissonance

495
00:20:48,800 --> 00:20:50,800
is as part and parcel

496
00:20:50,800 --> 00:20:52,800
of our work as anything else.

497
00:20:52,800 --> 00:20:54,800
this is a difficult thing to truly apply

498
00:20:54,800 --> 00:20:56,800
to your coaching style,

499
00:20:56,800 --> 00:20:58,800
to your Scouters style.

500
00:20:58,800 --> 00:21:00,800
on one hand, you have to be truly motivated

501
00:21:00,800 --> 00:21:02,800
and satisfied with incremental improvements,

502
00:21:02,800 --> 00:21:04,800
dissatisfied enough

503
00:21:04,800 --> 00:21:06,800
to sharpen the challenge

504
00:21:06,800 --> 00:21:08,800
to inspire and motivate the players

505
00:21:08,800 --> 00:21:10,800
towards reaching beyond themselves,

506
00:21:10,800 --> 00:21:12,800
and your effectiveness as a Scouter

507
00:21:12,800 --> 00:21:14,800
hinges on what you decide to do

508
00:21:14,800 --> 00:21:16,800
with the frustration this dissonance

509
00:21:16,800 --> 00:21:18,800
creates.

510
00:21:18,800 --> 00:21:20,800
we can turn this frustration into critical,

511
00:21:20,800 --> 00:21:22,800
cynical anger or into positive motivation.

512
00:21:22,800 --> 00:21:24,800
let's first do away

513
00:21:24,800 --> 00:21:26,800
with the idea that we're going to

514
00:21:26,800 --> 00:21:28,800
turn our frustration back on our Scouts

515
00:21:28,800 --> 00:21:30,800
in a negative way.

516
00:21:30,800 --> 00:21:32,800
this is a stereotypical coach's locker room

517
00:21:32,800 --> 00:21:34,800
speech that shames, berates

518
00:21:34,800 --> 00:21:36,800
and degrades the players.

519
00:21:36,800 --> 00:21:38,800
attitudes like that

520
00:21:38,800 --> 00:21:40,800
are iminical to our overall goal as Scouters.

521
00:21:40,800 --> 00:21:42,800
all of the tools in our toolbox

522
00:21:42,800 --> 00:21:44,800
are positive,

523
00:21:44,800 --> 00:21:46,800
just as the Scout Oath and Law is positive.

524
00:21:46,800 --> 00:21:48,800
we don't have to paint a negative picture

525
00:21:48,800 --> 00:21:50,800
of things.

526
00:21:50,800 --> 00:21:52,800
our Scouts are pretty good at doing that themselves.

527
00:21:52,800 --> 00:21:54,800
we always look for positive

528
00:21:54,800 --> 00:21:56,800
progress.

529
00:21:56,800 --> 00:21:58,800
we diffuse anger and disappointment

530
00:21:58,800 --> 00:22:00,800
and encouragement.

531
00:22:00,800 --> 00:22:02,800
angry, resentful, difficult

532
00:22:02,800 --> 00:22:04,800
coaches are sometimes pretty good coaches,

533
00:22:04,800 --> 00:22:06,800
but they've made the goal of the team, pleasing them

534
00:22:06,800 --> 00:22:08,800
of achieving their personal

535
00:22:08,800 --> 00:22:10,800
standard of achievement.

536
00:22:10,800 --> 00:22:12,800
they place themselves on a pedestal of importance.

537
00:22:12,800 --> 00:22:14,800
if you don't

538
00:22:14,800 --> 00:22:16,800
do as the coach says, the coach gets angry

539
00:22:16,800 --> 00:22:18,800
and is mean to you.

540
00:22:18,800 --> 00:22:20,800
but we know that really effective

541
00:22:20,800 --> 00:22:22,800
leaders, really effective

542
00:22:22,800 --> 00:22:24,800
coaches and Scouters

543
00:22:24,800 --> 00:22:26,800
imbue their followers with a sense of purpose

544
00:22:26,800 --> 00:22:28,800
and this motivates them

545
00:22:28,800 --> 00:22:30,800
to stretch themselves towards

546
00:22:30,800 --> 00:22:32,800
ever greater achievement.

